WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.140
“It's safe to say that Walter Cronkite was
not the most trusted man in America, and

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:11.140
it’s safe to say he was not even the 
most trusted man among newsmen.”

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:15.789
Hi I’m Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and
today we're talking with Joseph Campbell.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:19.410
He’s a Professor of Communications at
American University.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.580
The author of the great 2010
book “Getting it Wrong.”

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.910
Joe, thanks for talking with us! 
Thank you, thank you Nick. 
We're talking about

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.349
Walter Cronkite, there's a new biography
about Walter Cronkite and it kind

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:34.190
of trades in a couple of cliches about
Cronkite, specifically that he was the

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.670
most trusted man in America, and that his

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.810
statement about how we couldn’t win the
Vietnam War somehow turned the tide of

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:45.570
public opinion. What's wrong with our
understanding of Walter Cronkite? Well

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.590
we tend to put Walter Cronkite on a
pedestal and he was an esteemed

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:54.210
Newsman, who was the anchor for
CBS News for many years, from the

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.500
early 60’s until 1981

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.700
and there was a lot that Walter
Cronkite, I mean, he is a very familiar

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.930
figure in American households.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.360
But, was he the most trusted man in
America?

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.999
Sure he wasn’t I mean it's not that he was

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:10.369
distrusted, it's just that this is very
difficult to assess. 
Talk a little

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:14.609
bit about what, you know, when people were
pushed to produce evidence for

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:16.340
how we knew this. What,

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.280
what were the, what was the survey that
somehow dubbed him the most trusted

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:20.980
man?

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.930
This title, this epithet

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:30.350
can be traced to like 1972 and a
survey done by Quailing company

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.260
in 18 states of some eighty seven
hundred

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.750
respondents

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.350
tested the, the trust factor, what
they called the trust factor of

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.130
prominent, then prominent politicians;

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:46.900
Richard Nixon and Hubert Humphrey, Spiro
T. Agnew,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.920
the real sluggers of the hour. Yeah 
and then they threw in Walter

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.049
Cronkite’s name that’s 
sort of a barometer reading I guess,

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.729
and so compared to these,

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:00.260
skunks, yeah, 

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.390
Walter Cronkite, 

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.590
inevitably came up on top, so then
CBS News used this survey this with

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.200
the results of this survey which clearly
show Cronkite ahead of everybody else

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.620
I think in second place was the generic
US Senator and something.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.090
Richard Nixon's way down the list.
The generic, not Ted Kennedy, I think 

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.090
would have been down in the early 70's.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.930
I think Evan Musky finished third, 
in this ranking.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:23.680
But anyway, 

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.819
CBS News takes the survey results
and on the day of the election in

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:32.009
1972, in
November 1972

00:02:32.009 --> 00:02:35.809
runs full page ads in leading US
newspapers, New York Times, Chicago

00:02:35.809 --> 00:02:40.979
Tribune, Washington Post saying
re-elect the most trusted man in America. 

00:02:40.979 --> 00:02:44.659
and it was a way to tout Walter
Cronkite as the source to go to

00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:49.009
for election coverage, among the three
networks. 
In polls of the

00:02:49.009 --> 00:02:52.239
actual network anchors, how did Cronkite do?

00:02:52.239 --> 00:02:55.150
It fluctuated, there were surveys done
in the early

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.809
70’s by a polling organization called
Phillips Singlinger

00:02:58.809 --> 00:02:59.730
and one year

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.659
Cronkite’s no. 1, the next year
He is number 4. 

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:05.939
So there's some variation.

00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:09.549
So it's safe to say that Walter Cronkite
was not the most trusted man in

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:10.300
America,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.450
and it’s safe to say he was not even the  most trusted man
among newsman, among anchormen

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:14.649
Right. 

00:03:14.649 --> 00:03:17.949
in the early 70’s.
So, well let's talk
about this the

00:03:17.949 --> 00:03:22.869
apocryphal story that Lyndon
Johnson realizing after coverage of the

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:27.609
TET Offensive, He somehow says, “If I've
lost Cronkite, I've lost America.”

00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:30.150
What's wrong with that story, which we
all hear?

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.569
That's right! It's, It's one of the best
known anecdotes in American 

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:38.449
journalism and uh... it is uh... a
leading media myth. It’s a story that is

00:03:38.449 --> 00:03:42.279
delicious and appealing and widely
told but on close inspection

00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:43.979
dissolves as apocryphal or

00:03:43.979 --> 00:03:45.489
wildly exaggerated.

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:49.709
In this case, it’s very clear Lyndon
Johnson didn't even see the program.

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:55.009
..say that we are mired in stalemate seems
the only realistic, if unsatisfactory

00:03:55.009 --> 00:03:57.889
conclusion.” 
And the myth has it

00:03:57.889 --> 00:03:59.149
that- that Johnson

00:03:59.149 --> 00:04:01.529
watched this show in the White House.

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:03.899
And upon hearing Cronkite’s 

00:04:03.899 --> 00:04:07.709
declaration of the US military
affairs mired in stalemate,

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:10.959
Johnson leans over and snaps off the
television set and says something to the 

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:14.809
effect of  “If I’ve lost Cronkite, I've
lost middle America” or “If I've lost 

00:04:14.809 --> 00:04:17.239
Cronkite, I’ve lost the war” or “I’ve lost the country.”

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:18.389
The versions

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:21.969
vary markedly.  
And that's you said, that's a tell that

00:04:21.969 --> 00:04:24.840
in fact there wasn’t a single statement-
because if

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.930
there would be an actual quote
rather than these variations.
If, if anyone's

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.540
precise words should be captured,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.780
you'd expect them to be the President's.
We live in a world, where, you know, which

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.010
is saturated in media. I mean people can
access information emitted from a wide

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:42.409
variety of sources at any time of day.
You can push it, you can pull it. 

00:04:42.409 --> 00:04:46.380
And oddly, the media often looks at
this and panics but 

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:50.970
is it harder now to- yeah-I mean, is the
level of noise to information

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:56.470
higher than it used to be or, you know,
is this a good time for media from

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:00.380
a consumer's point of view? Or are we just
being fed lie after lie after lie?

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.830
You know I think it is a good time for
consumers. I mean, back when Cronkite was- was

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:05.889
on the air pontificating and so forth,

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:08.649
we had three options. Essentially three
network

00:05:08.649 --> 00:05:13.069
news programs, and now it's just 
all kinds of options. Cable over the

00:05:13.069 --> 00:05:18.399
air, etc et-cetera, online. This
variety can propel media myths in a

00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:20.560
pretty profound and rapid way.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.499
But the variety

00:05:22.499 --> 00:05:26.679
of options, also allows for these myths
to be knocked down fairly quickly.

00:05:26.679 --> 00:05:30.979
Well, I want to thank Joseph Campbell for
talking with Reason TV. You are

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:34.939
the author of “Getting it Wrong.” A
professor at American University.  Jo,

00:05:34.939 --> 00:05:37.620
thanks for talking to Reason. 
Nick, it was a pleasure! Thank you.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.580
And that’s the way it is!

